WEBVTT

longitude-caption.1.1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.233
How can a clock tell

longitude-caption.1.2
00:00:01.233 --> 00:00:02.767
a ship where it is?

longitude-caption.2.1
00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:04.400
By keeping the time

longitude-caption.2.2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.267
at a known reference point.

longitude-caption.3.1
00:00:06.533 --> 00:00:08.233
Sailors use the Sun

longitude-caption.3.2
00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:10.000
to find local time,

longitude-caption.3.3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.000
corrected to match

longitude-caption.3.4
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.333
the clock’s timekeeping.

longitude-caption.4.1
00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:13.767
Compare them

longitude-caption.4.2
00:00:13.767 --> 00:00:15.200
at the same moment.

longitude-caption.5.1
00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:17.267
Earth’s turning links

longitude-caption.5.2
00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:19.267
that time difference
to longitude:

longitude-caption.5.3
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:21.167
fifteen degrees per hour.

longitude-caption.6.1
00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:23.633
Twelve at the reference.

longitude-caption.6.2
00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:25.833
Two in the afternoon locally.

longitude-caption.7.1
00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:28.267
Two hours ahead.

longitude-caption.7.2
00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:29.833
Thirty degrees east.

longitude-caption.8.1
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.200
Harrison’s H4
helped demonstrate

longitude-caption.8.2
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.833
accurate timekeeping at sea.

longitude-caption.9.1
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:37.967
Other astronomical methods

longitude-caption.9.2
00:00:37.967 --> 00:00:39.867
remained useful too.

longitude-caption.10.1
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:42.600
The clock carries time.

longitude-caption.10.2
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.500
Observation and calculation

longitude-caption.10.3
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:45.800
reveal longitude.
